A||N||13||myblack86GT||A/C question||||1||
Z||000000||myblack86GT||09-04-2000||08:36 AM||||When I got my black 86 gt, I just about died when I found out it didnt have working air. I took it to a mechanic here, and her told me that there was a hose running from the front of the car to the back, and it has rubbed of the bottem of the car and worn holes in it, and would be close to $500 for parts and labor. I cant afford to spend that right now, so I got on the net and found this place for answers. Where can I get this part and is it really difficult to install? Also, if I do get it and install it, what can I do to prevent it from happening again? Thanks Guys||||reg||1
Z||000001||deceler8||09-04-2000||10:29 AM||||First of all, there are hoses running from the compressor in the rear of the car to the evaporator and condenser in the front and they are insulated (at least mine are)...is your mechanic seeing areas where the insulation is gone or is he seeing actual holes in the pipe ?<P>The A/C on cars this age fail for a variety of reasons...mine bit the dust due to a bad compressor. $500 to just fix the hole (assuming there is one) is pretty much rape...$500 to completely fix the system (parts and labor) is a pretty good price.<P>Another problem is the fact that Fiero systems use R12, which is now very pricey if you can get it at all. If you're gonna spend money to fix this, go to R134. There's an article by Ogre in the cave (see bottom of page for the link) regarding this switch.<P>Seems like for minimal money and time you could figure this out yourself with some compressed air and another set of ears to listen for the leak. A leak in one of the main lines (especially if you know where it should be) should make plenty of racket. <P>And it should cost much less than $500 to fix it. You just haven't taken it to the right place yet.||||reg||1
Z||000002||hugh||09-04-2000||11:20 AM||||First,check your lines visually.When you have a leak normally you will see signs of oil,unless it has been washed off by road travel through water.Compressed air is not a good idea,unless it has been dried,moisture is the worst enemy of a refrigeration system.You can get cans of refrigerant from places like pep boys,put pressure in the system and listen,but unless you have a bullet hole you probably won't hear it.If you can't hear it,or even if you can dilute some Dawn or other dish soap with water and use a squirt bottle to wet all the connections and any place you can get at.When you get any bubbles there is a leak.If it is a small leak it may take a while to show any foam(small bubbles)Look for grease around the compressor seal area(between the compressor pulley and body),that indicates a leak but unless you are prepared to replace the compressor ignore it.If you are thorough and find no leaks,leave the pressure in it and let it sit for a few hours(this assumes you have a pressure gauge of some sort on the system) if there is no pressure drop you can safely assume it is the seal.Don't worry about it,it will be cheaper to fill it once a year or so than to replace the compressor.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||myblack86GT||09-04-2000||11:32 AM||||Ok, according to him, he sees holes, according to me, I just see the insulation worn and torn. He told me that the tube would have to be replaced and couldnt be fixed.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> That $500 is only labor to replace the hose, and the cost of the hose (Which I believe he told me was around $250, just for the hose) If I do find a leak in it my self, how could I go about fixing it? Thanks for the help. Im also going to look into the cave place about replacing the system.||||reg||1
Z||000004||myblack86GT||09-04-2000||11:54 AM||||Iv been reading this info on the conversions, and was wondering, I would like to go this route, but where can I get everthing I need to do this. Right now, Im still not sure what all I need, new compressor? Is there a Fiero place that sells a kit or package to do this conversion, or should I have the shop I go to do it? Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000005||hugh||09-04-2000||02:23 PM||||I'm sure Pep Boys is a national chain. they sell a kit for about $35.00,complete.If you follow their directions I'm sure you'll be alright.Just be sure your system is tight.If you find a leak in a line,remove the line.I am not familar with the Fiero system,but the line must be removable.If it is a flexible rubber covered line,you may be able to find a transport refrigeration company near you like Thermo King or Carrier that uses that type of line for the small refrigerated trucks.If they have anybody worth their salt ,they can make you up a line or replace the bad section(it's not rocket science)If the section leaking is metal, that can be silver soldered.When the system is back together again go to a place that does car air conditioning and have them leak test your work and charge it for you.Just don't go to the thief who gave you that rediculous price. <p>[This message has been edited by hugh (edited 09-04-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||myblack86GT||09-04-2000||05:10 PM||||Ok that sounds good. I think Ill do that. There is a Pep Boys about 4 hours from here, I dont know if I could stand that drive until it cools down. Thanks for the help. I also thought that the price was a little high. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||hugh||09-04-2000||06:11 PM||||four hours?I wouldn't drive 4 hours for -nevermind.Seriously,there must be auto stores near you that sell a 134 conversion kit.Before you buy a kit find the leak and fix it.I don't know where you are located,but if you want to email me your phone number,if you get in trouble I'll call you and talk you through it.  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Monkeyman||09-04-2000||10:31 PM||||My mechanic said that it would take the replacement of the compressor for a proper R12-134a conversion.  Seemed kinda fishy to me as I've heard it done before with nothing more than a change of oil, but...Anyone know for sure?||||reg||1
Z||000009||mshill||09-04-2000||11:19 PM||||Monkey,  I'm not sure what your mechanic considers proper, but I just did the R134 conversion last Saturday in about an hour and it blows pretty cold.  I did have to replace the drier/accumulator because I had my system open for the engine rebuild.  The kit included an oil charge for the compressor that is supposed to have a seal conditioner in it.  The conversion was really easy and I think I paid $45 for it from Kragens (same as Checker).||||reg||1
Z||000010||Linx||09-05-2000||12:05 AM||||Walmart has the 134a retrofit kit for $34.  It's got high and low side adapters (they screw on to the old R12 fittings- this is the biggest part of the 134 "conversion"), 3 can's of 134a, oil, and a can-tap and hose.<P>Replacing the compressor aint necessary.<P>Provided your system is dry, leak and R12 free, it's an easy job.<p>[This message has been edited by Linx (edited 09-05-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Monkeyman||09-05-2000||06:06 AM||||It's not leak free yet (working on that).  How do I make sure it's R12 free?  It would save tons of money if I could do this on my own.  I know for sure that my compressor is in good shape.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Carrolles||09-05-2000||08:59 AM||||When you pull a vacuum on the system all of the old refrigerant will flash off.  Connect a vacuum pump to the low side fill fitting to do this.  Of course if you have R-12 you should have this done by a certified recycler so the freon does not escape into the atmosphere.||||reg||1
Z||000013||capt_ron||09-07-2000||02:45 AM||||STOP!!!<BR>Converting to R134a will cost you a lot of money in the long run. Especially if you use the low buck conversion kits. The oil from the R12 system is not compatable with the oil for R134a. If the system is not flushed completely and the compressor drained then the two oils will mix and "park" in the system. This will leave the compressor without lubication and it will self destruct in a matter of days. Also, no matter what you have heard, R134a is not as good as R12 and will not perform well in a system designed for R12. One big problem is the capacity of the condensor, R134a requires a larger condensor and there is no room to put one in a Fiero. The R134a requires higher pressures too and barrier hoses since the molecules are smaller and will leak easier. This is just not a simple conversion and you will be disappointed.<BR>As far as the leaking line under the car, you can buy the hose to replace this by the foot. By reusing the ends you can use special A/C hose clamps to make the line yourself. You will have to use a hacksaw to remove the crimped collars but after that the ends will pull right out. You will also have to replace the accumilator in the front compartment. Any time that a system is opened it has to be evacuated by pulling a vacumm on it for at least 30 minutes. This is required to remove the moisture in the system since the mixture of water and R12 forms acids that eat away the aluminum parts. Moisture will also form ice inside the pipes and block them. Pull the vacumm though both the high and low ports and check for major leaks by removing the vacumm source and letting the system sit for a while. If the vacumm remains then you can start charging the system with R12.<BR>Pressurizing the system with air to look for leaks is bad too, this can form an explosive mixture...<P>Take a look on the Automotive A/C Bulletin Board  <A HREF="http://www.aircondition.com/wwwboard/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.aircondition.com/wwwboard/</A> <BR>There you will find one R134a conversion horror story after another.<P>Good Luck  <P>------------------<BR>CAPT RON...<BR>Jack of Trades,<BR>Master of None.<BR>87 SE, 2.8L, Getrag.<P>[This message has been edited by capt_ron (edited 09-07-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by capt_ron (edited 09-07-2000).]||||reg||1
